#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "cbd0cb3d-cf0f-54db-8cfc-c938e463e4f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5087" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n$x=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0$." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t$. First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2$:\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting\n\n$$\nt^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\ninto our expression for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= t^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nt \\geq0\n$$\n\nand $x=2t^2$, we must have\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "f3d396ea-caed-51ed-a4d8-0bfc3301f2f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5101" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}x = \\csc   t \\, & \\Rightarrow  \\, \\csc   t = x \\\\ y = 2 \\cot   t \\, & \\Rightarrow  \\, \\cot   t = \\frac{y}{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1\n$$\n\nby $\\sin^{2}{t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t} + \\frac{\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} t} \\\\\n1 + (\\frac{\\cos t}{\\sin t})^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{\\sin t})^{2} \\\\\n1 + \\cot^{2} t &= \\csc^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and\n\n$$\n\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}\n$$\n\ninto this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + (\\frac{y}{2})^{2} &= x^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} &= x^{2} - 1 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(x^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is\n\n$$\ny^2 = 4\\left(x^2-1 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand $x=\\csc t$, the interval on the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ac2898-7d60-58c9-b877-aa6227161d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{1 - 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098509} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac6f5f2-d9b6-5628-9395-de2646e703c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{1 + 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098510} {:answer/id #uuid "d885f9bc-d333-5c26-94ca-f091a0146843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{-1 - 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098511} {:answer/id #uuid "0de38c6c-73ed-505c-840c-7effcf16fed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = - (\\frac{1 + 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098512} {:answer/id #uuid "524dabc5-8fef-57c2-94e3-cb9f7ccf3962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = - (\\frac{-1 + 2x}{x})^{2}, x \\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ac6f5f2-d9b6-5628-9395-de2646e703c4" :answer/type {:db/ide